Subject: [PATCH 58/58] linuxbios_info: the firmware's own walk, re-run and watched

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

The three words exist on x86 and amd64 only -- CONFIG_LINUXBIOS is false
everywhere else, and those two are also the only arch/*/lib.c with the bump
allocator behind heap-cursor. ppc and unix are on that line because two of the
seven files are SHARED HEADERS they compile, so "it builds and its tracks pass
there" is a claim this lab can make and therefore has to: the whole suite was
run on all four.

B.3's last unmet success-signature line (plan §9, Spike 3) is "a region diff
shows a FIRMWARE-CAUSED change". The word doing the work is *firmware-caused*:
a diff over bytes the test itself wrote proves the instrument and nothing else.
So the subject has to be a code path already in the tree, and this is the one
the plan names -- libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c, the coreboot-table parser that
runs at init and that this lab has already patched twice (01: publish the real
memory map; 39: chase LB_TAG_FORWARD into CBMEM).

Three words, bound the way patches 14/16 established (after device_end(), or a
global bind is not FOUND):

    lb-table    ( -- phys len )   the CBMEM-forwarded table, header included
    lb-walk     ( -- n )          re-run the parse into a SCRATCH sys_info;
                                  n = RAM ranges written, -1 = no table
    heap-cursor ( -- phys avail ) where arch/{x86,amd64}/lib.c's bump allocator
                                  will put the NEXT malloc

THE REVIEW THAT SCHEDULED THIS WORK WAS WRONG ABOUT WHERE THE CHANGE LANDS, and
publishing lb-table is how that gets said out loud rather than quietly dropped.
F5/F6 read: "snapshot the CBMEM-forwarded table region, re-run the walk, diff".
Measured 2026-09-03: read_lbtable() is a READER. That region comes back
byte-identical every time (LBTAB=0), so it is the NEGATIVE CONTROL, not the
subject. The write is one level down -- convert_memmap() calls malloc() and
fills what it gets -- which is why heap-cursor exists at all: it is the only
way to snapshot bytes BEFORE the firmware writes them, and afterwards there is
nothing to compare against.

ADDRESSES OUT ARE PHYSICAL, ON PURPOSE. x86 relocates by rebasing the GDT
(arch/x86/segment.c), so a bare firmware pointer handed to Forth would let a
snapshot land on memory that READS BACK CONVINCINGLY -- no error, plausible
bytes, and a diff of zero, which is the failure mode that looks like success.
Physical forces the caller through dsl/struct.fth's >virt, and the `region-diff`
track measures the trap in both directions: 0 on x86 without >virt, and equal to
the >virt path on amd64, where virt_offset is 0 and there is no trap to bite.

lb_find_table() is collect_linuxbios_info()'s own search, unchanged, lifted out
so it can be run more than once. collect_linuxbios_info() keeps its behaviour
exactly; lb-walk never touches the live sys_info.

x86 and amd64 only: CONFIG_LINUXBIOS is false on ppc/sparc32/sparc64/ppc64, and
those two are also the only arch/*/lib.c with the bump allocator heap-cursor
reports (sparc64's malloc is ofmem_malloc, which has no such cursor).

Measured after, on both, with QEMU's monitor reading the same guest-physical
bytes from outside: LBTAB=0, the cursor advances 0x90 (nine 16-byte memranges),
seven bytes change, all inside that block, and they decode to the two RAM ranges
of the machine QEMU was given (0x1000+0x9f000 and 0x100000+0x1fd71000 = 510 MiB
of -m 512). Three negative controls were re-injected and watched to bite: a
region-diffs that always answers 0 (SELFTEST goes to 0), a region-snap that
forgets >virt (HEAP goes to 0 on x86 while SELFTEST stays 1, so the message
correctly points at the subject and not the instrument), and a lb-walk that
skips read_lbtable (RANGES goes to 0).

All seven files, in one diff: the two `arch/*/lib.c` hunks are identical because
the two bump allocators are copies of each other, and so are the two
`arch/*/openbios.c` hunks, for the same reason.

diff --git a/include/libc/stdlib.h b/include/libc/stdlib.h
--- a/include/libc/stdlib.h
+++ b/include/libc/stdlib.h
@@ -20,6 +20,18 @@
 extern void	free( void *ptr );
 extern void	*realloc( void *ptr, size_t size );
 
+/* -- mklab, B.3 Spike 3 (patch 58): the allocator's own cursor ---------------
+ * NOT libc. arch/{x86,amd64}/lib.c implement malloc as a bump pointer over a
+ * static 128 KiB array and free() as a no-op, so "where will the next malloc
+ * write" is a question those two arches can answer exactly -- which is what a
+ * region-diff needs in order to snapshot bytes BEFORE the firmware's own code
+ * path writes them. Physical, because the snapshot address has to survive
+ * x86's relocation the way every other address in this lab does (>virt).
+ * Only the bump allocators define these; sparc64's malloc is ofmem_malloc and
+ * has no such cursor, and nothing outside CONFIG_LINUXBIOS calls them. */
+extern unsigned long	malloc_cursor_phys( void );
+extern unsigned long	malloc_avail( void );
+
 /* should perhaps go somewhere else... */
 extern void	qsort( void *base, size_t nmemb, size_t size, int (*compar)(const void*, const void*));
 
diff --git a/arch/x86/lib.c b/arch/x86/lib.c
--- a/arch/x86/lib.c
+++ b/arch/x86/lib.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 
 #include "config.h"
 #include "asm/types.h"
+#include "asm/io.h"		/* virt_to_phys, for malloc_cursor_phys (patch 58) */
 #include <stdarg.h>
 #include "libc/stdlib.h"
 #include "libc/vsprintf.h"
@@ -54,3 +55,23 @@
 {
 	/* Nothing yet */
 }
+
+/* -- mklab, B.3 Spike 3 (patch 58): where the NEXT allocation will write ------
+ * The allocator above is a bump pointer over a static array with a free() that
+ * does nothing, so `memptr` is not an implementation detail to be guessed at --
+ * it is the exact address the next malloc() returns. Publishing it is what lets
+ * a Forth region-diff snapshot those bytes BEFORE the firmware's own coreboot-
+ * table parser (libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c) allocates and writes there, and
+ * then show the change the firmware itself caused rather than one the test made.
+ * PHYSICAL on purpose: on x86 a Forth address is the relocated one, and handing
+ * out a bare pointer would let a snapshot land on RAM that reads back
+ * convincingly -- the trap dsl/struct.fth's >virt/>phys pair exists for. */
+unsigned long malloc_cursor_phys(void)
+{
+	return virt_to_phys(memptr);
+}
+
+unsigned long malloc_avail(void)
+{
+	return (unsigned long)memsize;
+}
